<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somba Opu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Somba Opu was the fortified royal capital and port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-gowa">Gowa Sultanate</a> near modern Makassar in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>. Its brick walls enclosed palaces and merchant quarters used by Malay, Portuguese, English and Danish traders. It was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> allies in 1669 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makassar-war">Makassar War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somba-opu.jpg" alt="Somba Opu" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Si Gam · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benteng_Somba_Opu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Somba Opu stood near the mouth of the Jeneberang River, close to Kale Gowa, one of the two oldest areas of Makassar, whose walls held the coronation stone of the rulers, a sacred spring and royal tombs. In the 16th century the karaeng Tunipalangga (1548–1566) allowed Malay merchants to set up a trading port at Mangallekana, and he then ordered a new fortress built on the north bank of the Jeneberang, about a kilometre from its mouth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Somba Opu became the heart of Makassar under the long duumvirate of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> of Gowa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaeng-matoaya">Karaeng Matoaya</a> of Tallo (1590–1637), who added brick forts at Tallo', Panakukkang and Ujung Pandang, the later Fort Rotterdam. It was the commercial capital, while old Gowa remained a ceremonial centre. A royal complex of two great wooden palaces, storehouses and a mosque stood in its south-west, with markets outside the walls and the quarters of Portuguese, Indian and other European traders along the coast.</p><p>After the Bungaya Treaty of 1667, Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-speelman">Cornelis Speelman</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> gathered 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> soldiers, men from Ternate, Tidore and other islands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> troops. On 14 June 1669 explosives in a tunnel opened a breach of about 27.5 meters, which the defenders closed with stakes. On 22 June the Bugis prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arung-palakka">Arung Palakka</a> led his men into the breach, and fire fanned by the wind drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-hasanuddin">Sultan Hasanuddin</a> from the fort. Some 8,000 Bugis then looted it, and the Dutch removed its guns, among them 33 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The bricks were reused for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> buildings and local houses, and the growing delta buried the ruins until their rediscovery in the 1980s; the fort was reconstructed in 1990. It was a rectangle of about 240 x 500 meters, and the best-preserved part is a red brick western wall 3.5 meters thick, ending in the bastion called Maccini' Sombala'. A 9-meter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and other finds are kept in a nearby site museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fort Somba Opu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fortified port of <b>Somba Opu</b> became its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Gowa Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
